  "final_text": "I finished all four steps, and the rule reads back as enabled.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Logs' (index `kibana_sample_data_logs`) uses `timestamp` as its time field. It also has `@timestamp` and `utc_time` date fields. The response code is a text field, `response`, with a keyword version, `response.keyword`.\n2. **Dashboard:** I created 'S4-2052af8d Web logs' with two panels: a line chart of the request count over time and a bar chart of the request count by `response.keyword`.\n3. **Alert rule:** I created 'S4-2052af8d 5xx errors' already enabled, so it didn't need enabling afterwards. It runs every minute against `kibana_sample_data_logs`, uses `timestamp` as its time field, and fires when more than 10 matching requests arrive within 5 minutes. A request matches when its `response.keyword` is between \"500\" and \"599\". The codes are stored as text, so that range is alphabetical; it covers every 5xx code but would also catch odd values like \"5000\", which aren't expected in this data.\n4. **Panel edit and check:** I renamed the response-code panel to 'Responses by code', and the dashboard shows the new title. Reading the rule back gives `enabled: true` with status `ok`.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
